  "name": "Investigations on Shallow Subtidal Habitats and Assemblages in Lower Cook Inlet",
  "description": "This report references population abundance data of algal species: Alaria taeniata, Laminaria groenlandica, Nereocystis luetkeana, fish species (Hexagrammos decagrammus), and invertebrate species: Clinocardium nuttali, Evasterias troschelii, Macoma spp., Modiolus modiolus, Mya truncata, Saxidomus giganteus, Siliqua patula, Spisula polynyma, Strongylocentrotus droebachiensis, and Telmessus cheiragonus; diet data of invertebrate species: Dermasterias imbricata, Evasterias troschelii, Macoma spp., Modiolus modiolus, Pycnopodia helianthoides, Saxidomus giganteus, and Telmessus cheiragonus; geographic and size distribution data of invertebrate species (Modiolus modiolus); and species composition data of algal species: Alaria taeniata, Laminaria groenlandica, Nereocystis luetkeana, fish species (Hexagrammos decagrammus), and invertebrate species: Clinocardium nuttali, Evasterias troschelii, Modiolus modiolus, Mya truncata, Saxidomus giganteus, Strongylocentrotus droebachiensis, and Telmessus cheiragonus from Archimandritof Shoals, Barabara Point, Black Reef, Bluff Point, Cottonwood Bay, Gull Island, Homer, Iliamna Bay, Iniskin Bay, Jakolof Bay, Knoll Head Lagoon, Mud Bay, Oil Bay, Scott Island, Seldovia Point, Troublesome Creek, and White Gull Island, Cook Inlet, Alaska. The aforementioned data are referenced in citation: Lees, D. C. and W. B. Driskell. 1981. Investigations on shallow subtidal habitats and assemblages in lower Cook Inlet. Environmental Assessment of the Alaskan Continental Shelf. final Reports of Principal Investigators. Biological Studies, NOAA/OMPA, Boulder, CO (USA) , 14: 417-610. This literature was collected as part of a metadatabase that represents one or more nearshore resources (from physical attributes such as water and air, to birds and mammals). Each entry is viewable in three formats, 1) a geospatial explicit format (ArcView 3.3) named GIS ArcView 3.3 Near GEM project, 2) a spreadsheet format (Excel 2002 and within ArcView) named GEM Nearshore Metadata Set, and 3) a ProCite database named GEM Nearshore Metadata References, which consists of references included in 1 and 2 above. The ProCite database record number corresponds to the Excel Procite Number. Reference Procite #595 for this literature's metadata. The metadata are maintained in digital format (ArcView 3.3, Excel 2002, and Procite) and are available from the Alaska Science Center, USGS in Anchorage, Alaska. For a detailed description of the three databases and the project, see \"Bodkin, J. L., and T. Dean. 2003. Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project G-030687), US Geological Survey, Alaska Science Center, Anchorage, Alaska.\" available on-line at http://www.evostc.state.ak.us/pdf/GEM_final_reports/030687.pdf.\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: Part of the design effort of the \"Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring\" (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project). Provides a comprehensive historical perspective of locations and types of past studies conducted in the nearshore marine communities within Gulf of Alaska in the form of a geographical information system database.",
  "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/23571576",
